<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teimuraz I of Kakheti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1589">1589</a>–<a href="/years/1663">1663</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Teimuraz I was king of Kakheti in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> from 1605, with interruptions, and later also of Kartli, as well as a noted poet. His long resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> led to the devastation of Kakheti in 1614–1617, when many thousands of people were killed or deported, and to the martyrdom of his mother Ketevan. He spent his last years as a prisoner in Iran.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teimuraz-i-of-kakheti.jpg" alt="Teimuraz I of Kakheti" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domainPublic domainfalsefalse This work is in the public domain in its country of origin and other countries and areas … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Teimuraz_I_with_Queen_Khoreshan,_by_Castelli.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Teimuraz was the eldest son of David I of Kakheti and Ketevan, and a grandson of Alexander II, whom his own son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> had killed in an Iranian-sponsored coup. When Christian Kakhetians rallied by Ketevan overthrew Constantine, the nobles asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a> to confirm Teimuraz, and the shah, at war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, agreed. Later, having refused a summons to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, Teimuraz sent his mother and his sons Levan and Alexander to the shah as hostages, but in 1614 Abbas, aided by the defector Giorgi Saakadze, drove him into Imereti.</p><p>On 15 September 1615 Kakhetian nobles under David Jandieri rose against the shah's appointee <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isa-khan">Isa Khan</a> and proposed Teimuraz as king of all eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. His forces routed an Iranian army at Tsitsamuri, but Abbas came in person in 1616; towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gremi">Gremi</a> and Zagem shrank to villages, and the descendants of the more than one hundred thousand peasants deported make up the bulk of the ethnic Georgian population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. In 1625 Saakadze turned against Iran, won the Battle of Martkopi and restored Teimuraz as king of Kartli and Kakheti, and in 1626 Teimuraz defeated Saakadze at Bazaleti.</p><p>His embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-spain">Philip IV of Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-viii">Pope Urban VIII</a> produced only the first printed Georgian book, a Georgian-Italian dictionary (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, 1629). Sheltering Daud Khan cost him both kingdoms in 1633 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safi-of-persia">Shah Safi</a>'s favourite Rostom. Pardoned and restored to Kakheti in 1638, he swore allegiance to Tsar Michael in 1639, but in 1648 Rostom routed him at Magharo, where his last surviving son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> fell.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Educated at the Safavid court, Teimuraz wrote a Georgian full of Persian imagery and loanwords. In 1629–34 he translated and adapted Persian romances, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/layla-and-majnun">Layla and Majnun</a>, Yusuf and Zulaikha, The Rose and the Nightingale and The Candle and the Moth. His first poem, The Book and Passion of Queen Ketevan (1625), drew on the Augustinian missionaries who witnessed her death; Donald Rayfield wrote that whatever <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> lost in the king, it gained in the poet. In exile in 1649–56 he mourned his kingdom and cursed the bloodthirsty king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After failing to win Russian help, though he visited Tsar Alexis in June 1658, Teimuraz retired to a monastery in 1661. Vakhtang V sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-ii-of-persia">Abbas II</a> received him with honour but imprisoned him after his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> tried to take Kakheti. He died in captivity at the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Astarabad</a>, and his remains were buried at the Alaverdi Monastery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Teimuraz I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Teimuraz I</b>'s resistance provoked Shah Abbas I to devastate the country in 1616, deporting many thousands of Kakhetians to Iran; Teimuraz's mother Ketevan was martyred in 1624.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kakheti">Kingdom of Kakheti</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Gorgan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.72,44.79&amp;year=1637" aria-label="Tbilisi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5641 677 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Teimuraz I of Kakheti"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6091 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6091 947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.72,44.79&amp;year=1637">What was here in 1637? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
